  "generated_text": "1. Given \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we need to find \\( f^{-1} \\).\n2. Let \\( f(x) = y \\). Therefore, \\( y = q(p(r(x))) \\).\n3. To find \\( f^{-1} \\), we need to express \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\).\n4. Start with \\( y = q(p(r(x))) \\).\n5. Apply the inverse of \\( q \\) to both sides: \\( q^{-1}(y) = p(r(x)) \\).\n6. Apply the inverse of \\( p \\) to both sides: \\( p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y)) = r(x) \\).\n7. Apply the inverse of \\( r \\) to both sides: \\( r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) = x \\).\n\nThus, \\( f^{-1}(y) = r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) \\).\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is \\( r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\)",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[c, '\\\\text{C}']",
    "gold_answer": "Let $y = f(x) = q(p(r(x))).$  Applying $q^{-1},$ we get\n\\[q^{-1}(y) = p(r(x)).\\]Applying $p^{-1},$ we get\n\\[p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y)) = r(x).\\]Finally, applying $r^{-1}(x),$ we get\n\\[r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) = x.\\]Hence, $f^{-1} = r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}.$  The correct answer is $\\boxed{\\text{C}}.$",
    "is_correct": true
  },
